WEBVTT

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.810
Imagine you could travel back in time.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.800
to a time long before man.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.070
back across 65 million years.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.940
As you travel, you would see huge changes in the vegetation and the climate.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.580
Even the surface of the Earth itself would move.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.830
as mountain rangers are pushed up by colliding continents.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.980
We'll be right back.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.260
Now you've reached a remarkable period in Earth's history.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:15.840
known as the Cretaceous.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.110
It is a very different world.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.500
The Himalayas do not yet exist.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.220
and the Atlantic is only half as wide.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.050
There is no grass.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.880
only conifer forests and fern prairies

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:31.990
This is a world.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.080
Ruled.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:34.310
by dinosaurs.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.440
Thank you.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.530
Giant reptiles like Tyrannosaurus, a five-ton predator,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.180
stalk the landscape.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.480
walking with dinosaurs.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:01:59.860
We will show you.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
how these magnificent creatures live.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:03.550
How they eat.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:04.530
Fight.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:05.710
and reproduce.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.180
Thank you.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.350
and you will witness how the forces of nature

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:11.550
conspire

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.220
to drive these animals to extinction.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.600
This series will also take you back much further.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.160
Back to the Jurassic period.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.180
A time when life on earth

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.990
was at its most spectacular.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:37.690
You

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.420
Thank you.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.880
The creatures here bathe in a warm tropical climate.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.380
There are no ice caps at the poles.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.940
Flowers and broad-leaved trees are yet to evolve.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.640
in the air and on the land.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.370
The world is dominated by reptiles.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:02:59.950
and by far the most

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.320
common.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:01.760
are the dinosaurs.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.100
But they are not the only giants.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.330
Huge pterosaurs rule the skies.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.750
and below them

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.580
Massive marine reptiles harvest the rich oceans.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:37.470
However, first this series will go back even further to discover where dinosaurs came from.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:44.500
This is our own Earth during the Triassic period.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.500
Here there are no separate continents, just one giant landmass called Pangaea.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.950
It is a harsh place, dominated by deserts.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:09.100
The Triassic has already seen many different varieties of ancient reptiles come and go,

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.860
but now out of this dry wilderness has appeared something revolutionary.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:22.570
A family of reptiles destined to shape the course of life on Earth for the next 160 million years.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.740
These are the first dinosaurs, and this is where our story begins.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:50.880
First light across the western hills of Earth's only continent, Pangaea.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:57.520
This world has been ruled by one group of giant reptiles for over 50 million years.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.970
But these ancient creatures...

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:09.620
have had their day all over these lush fern prairies a grim evolutionary battle has broken out

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:19.860
among the newer types of reptile for supremacy of this strange world on these triassic proving

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:26.370
grounds dinosaurs are still comparatively rare but they are beginning to show the first signs of

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.630
their future success

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:43.120
It is the end of the wet season, and the local river is full, but it will not remain this lush,

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.150
and ahead lies nine months with no rain.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.320
One type of reptile has evolved to thrive in drought.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.940
Dinosaurs like this Coelophysis can survive on very little water.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.970
She is also light-boned.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:26.120
fast and she is beautifully adapted for killing dinosaurs first appeared around 10 million years

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.700
before as small predators but what makes them unique

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:35.100
is special hips and ankles that allow them to stand perfectly balanced on two legs.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:40.640
With lightning-fast reactions, they are built to survive.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.260
Growing chorus of calls signals the arrival of a huge herd of placerias.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:58.790
They are making their way down from the fern scrubland for their morning drink.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.390
These impressive one-ton beasts are not related to dinosaurs.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.520
They are a much more ancient type of reptile.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.720
Once, there were many different varieties of these powerful creatures swaggering across the landscape.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:25.050
But now the Placereus are the only ones of their kind that remain.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:27.600
They are an endangered species.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:29.960
Despite their fearsome...

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:35.620
They are actually gentle herbivores their tasks are used for digging up roots

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:44.480
But on two angry males these tasks can make lethal weapons

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:59.980
For the swift seal of faces plus serious are prey this year

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:24.780
female targets the old and weak in the slow-moving herb. One day, the descendants of dinosaurs

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.880
like Coelophysis will take over this world, but it is their speed and agility

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.440
that gives them the edge in the mid-Triassic.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.600
Further downriver is one of the Triassic's most bizarre animals.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.320
The Cynodont is a missing link between reptiles and mammals.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.470
As he runs, his backbone moves from side to side like a reptile's,

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.210
but he has hair and lives down a burrow like a mammal.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.100
Deep inside, his mate sleeps on a bed of lichen.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.320
The bond between cynodonts is extraordinarily strong.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.160
They pair for life.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.690
Like all land reptiles, they lay eggs.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.880
But after hatching, the young are utterly dependent on their parents

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.190
and spend their first three months feeding from special milk glands on the mother's stomach.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:35.760
This form of reproduction has evolved to protect their young from the daylight predators outside.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:45.460
By day, even the father sticks close to the safety of the burrow and carries out domestic chores.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.560
He only hunts at night.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.980
In the not-too-distant...

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:16.890
future, small furry mammals will evolve from reptiles like these. In the heat of

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:22.070
the dry season, the Placirias herd spreads out over the scrubland to feed.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.210
Suddenly the warm breeze changes.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.960
and there is the scent of fear in the air.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:10.120
Postasuchus, a merciless ambush predator, the largest carnivore on earth.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:23.440
The terrified Placereus flee, but they are desperately slow, and one of them now carries a mortal wound.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.410
Their tormentor is a distant cousin of the dinosaurs, evolved from the same reptilian ancestors.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.470
But unlike them, she is too front-heavy to run on two legs.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:38.200
Yet the postasuchus is easily fast enough to keep pace with the herd.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:47.000
Eventually, a combination of shock and blood loss defeats the wounded Placerius.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.800
Carnivore's bite has done its job.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:09.320
The Placereus is now too weak to fight, and the Postosuchus has an easy meal.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:25.150
As the weeks of the dry season pass, only the vegetation around the river remains lush,

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:29.970
and this attracts exotic hunters from far and wide, like this...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.980
is Pettinosaurus. She is a flying reptile who has evolved extra long fingers to support

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:51.470
her delicate wing membranes. The river's resident flying predators are dragonflies. Insects

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:57.440
like these evolved long before the first reptiles and over 100 million years ago some took to

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.690
the air and became aerial killers.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:05.750
But in the Triassic era, these hunters have become the hunted.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.960
A jaw full of needle-sharp teeth makes short work of the insect.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:38.400
Ptinosaurus have evolved strong, lightweight bones for flight and like the dinosaurs, they are fast and deadly.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.960
Reptiles are becoming the new masters of the air.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:53.610
In temperatures of over 40 degrees centigrade, the placerias need lots of water

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:58.710
and these ancient reptiles have to spend more time at the river, but the herd is nervous.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:14.990
a good place for an ambush. The postasuchus is not hunting. She has recently eaten her

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:25.390
fill and also needs to come down to the river to drink. Six meters long, heavily built with

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:29.980
an armored back, she needs a huge amount of food and therefore has to...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:40.460
defend a very large territory. The only creature on the planet she fears is

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:59.860
another Postosuchus. It is now the middle

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.900
of the dry season, and months since any rain. Across the scrub land, smaller rivers are

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:18.150
drying out. In one, a Ptynosaurus risks a cooling bath. He constantly checks for danger.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:38.340
The cynodonts lie secure and cool through the hottest part of the day, but no animal here is truly safe.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.660
The female Coelophysis has picked up the scent of their bedding.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:51.200
Soon she is exploring the entrance, and she is not alone.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.540
Clearly, these dinosaurs have not met cynodonts before.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:22.680
Nearby, the placerias search for moist roots beneath the drying ferns.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:32.620
The postasuchus once again needs food.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.420
But her last attack left her with a tusk wound on her thigh.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.740
a severe handicap for such a heavily built creature.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:52.250
The Placirias herd spots her before she can set an ambush.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.280
and they mount an aggressive defense.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.600
She will get no food here.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.720
And in such a competitive world,

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:04.270
Her wound may yet prove fatal.

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:08.630
Evening.

00:17:09.590 --> 00:17:10.450
and in the burrow.

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:13.060
The Cynodonts prepare for a night of hunting.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:16.690
The young are more developed now.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:18.630
and starting to move about the nest.

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:20.360
but they are still vulnerable.

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:24.770
And it will be another two months before they can do without their parents' protection.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:33.620
The adult male checks the coast is clear.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:50.740
An inquisitive youngster follows him to the end of the burrow.

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:03.620
Ah

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.040
Too late, the male responds to his squealing pop.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:25.480
Once again, he drives the dinosaurs away.

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:27.220
But this time.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:28.400
They don't go far.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:31.090
Thank you.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:31.680
Thank you.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:32.920
Nearby.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:37.310
A thirsty male postosuchus has invaded the wounded female's territory.

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:43.940
But this withered Triassic landscape cannot support two giant predators in one dry valley.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:09.910
The female is too weak to fight.

00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:15.830
dominating her territory for a decade.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:18.020
She is driven from her home.

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:21.360
The male uses precious water.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.000
to mark his new territory.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:36.760
Meanwhile, the Coelophyses are starting to dig the cynodonts out.

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:43.100
Their situation is becoming desperate.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.260
as the dinosaurs will soon expose the nest.

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.460
as the sun sets.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:19:59.970
The pair have only one choice.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:07.070
It means shattering their unique parental bond.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.320
They eat their own young.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:12.180
This denies the dinosaurs their food

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.820
and gives the cynodonts the chance of escape.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:27.780
Under the moonlight, they seize the moment

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:28.980
and abandon the whole.

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:44.770
In the morning, the coelophysis are back.

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.490
they can still smell the cynodonts.

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:51.260
and it will take them some time to realize their work is in vain.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:09.730
The dry season continues.

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:13.050
After her wasted effort at the Cynodont hole,

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.850
The female Coelophysis has found something in the parched riverbed.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:18.630
It is a lung fish.

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.250
and the dinosaur's unique serrated teeth.

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:24.020
will make short work of its protective cocoon.

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.640
but she is not left alone to eat it for long.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:39.760
Coelophysis numbers are on the increase.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:42.140
and this leads to confrontations.

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:04.730
The pressure of numbers also brings other changes in behaviour.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:11.840
Under the Merciless Sun

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:14.210
A flock of Coelophyses has united.

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:15.310
for a kill.

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:20.750
The wounded female Pustasuchus has lost the use of her back legs.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:25.510
However, her mighty jaws could still tear a dinosaur apart.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:50.560
carnivore's strength fails.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:56.920
all the Postasuchus' heavy armor and muscle.

00:22:57.420 --> 00:22:59.940
are no more than food for the agile little.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:00.520
dinosaurs.

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:03.860
their long snouts and nibbling front teeth.

00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:06.010
can reach under her thick scales.

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:07.520
They eat her.

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:08.940
from the inside out.

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:10.570
Thank you.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:36.510
The rains are late.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.680
And now the real test for survival begins.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:43.280
as vast areas turn into red desert.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:45.980
The Placereas are driven to migrate.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:47.400
in search of water.

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:01.810
But in this extreme drought,

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:05.510
dinosaurs have a crucial advantage over other reptiles.

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:07.400
when they excrete.

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:09.230
they waste very little water.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:17.000
Nowhere is this advantage more obvious than down by the shrunken river.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.170
Coelophysis are gathering in huge numbers.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:24.230
And in years of hardship like this, it is the dinosaurs that win true.

00:24:25.340 --> 00:24:27.540
There is little room for any other reptiles here.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.600
and without sufficient prey.

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:32.810
The dinosaurs swiftly turn on each other.

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:37.170
Cannibalism is common.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:45.060
The Triassic is pushing life to its limits.

00:24:56.230 --> 00:24:59.970
Night reveals dinosaurs are not the only animals that have managed to...

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:00.560
Klingon.

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:05.090
The Cynodonts have survived their brush with death.

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:08.560
and dug another burrow hidden in some dried ferns.

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:11.970
Once again, the male is hunting in the darkness.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.270
He has caught the only common prey.

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:26.660
baby coelophysis.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:35.900
Thank you.

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:47.060
after nine months of drought.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:49.680
At last, the rains have arrived.

00:26:24.860 --> 00:26:26.740
thirsty scrublands are soaking.

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:45.800
Inside her burrow, the female cynodont has laid a fresh clutch of eggs.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:50.560
One day in the distant future, this strategy of investing in their young

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:52.740
will pay off for their mammalian descendants.

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:57.110
But as mammals evolve over the next 160 million years,

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.940
They will only survive as a few small species.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:00.200
cities.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:02.240
clinging on at the edge of a world.

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:04.000
dominated by dinosaurs.

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:11.040
Outside, that future is already arriving.

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:17.850
The female Coelophysis has survived the drought.

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:20.320
along with many of her kind.

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:28.210
but they have also been joined by another type of dinosaur.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:46.370
A huge herd of platiosaurus has been drawn to the swollen river.

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:51.360
It is hard to believe that these four-legged beasts are related to Coelophysis.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:53.980
But these are plant-eating dinosaurs.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.000
Their size is the key to their success.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:00.320
success.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:01.860
and at four tons.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:04.630
They are simply too big to be threatened.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:12.320
This is the shape of things to come.

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:14.740
The Age of the Dinosaurs.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.100
has dawned.

00:28:43.910 --> 00:28:48.830
20 million years later, a massive extinction wiped out thousands of species of reptile.

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:51.740
But the dinosaurs went from strength to strength.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:54.500
Carnivores grew to enormous sizes.

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:56.870
But their prey got even bigger.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:58.980
in the next program.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.160
we meet the giants of the Jurassic.
